'''after''' (ȧf′tėr), ''a.'' [< ME. ''after'', ''æfter'', etc., adj., merged with ''after'', adv., in loose comp. like ''after-past'', etc.; < AS. ''æftera'', fem. and neut. ''æftere'', adj., < ''æfter'', adv. and prep.] '''1.''' Later in time; subsequent; succeeding: as, an ''after'' period of life. {{smaller|[''After'' in composition may be either the adjective in loose combination, where the hyphen is optional: as, an ''after period'', ''after-ages''; or the adverb, qualifying a verbal form, or depending logically on a verb implied: as, ''after-past'', the ''aftercome'', ''aftergrowth''. The loose combinations are very numerous; only a few are here given.]}}
{{smaller block|<poem>So smile the Heavens upon this holy act
That ''after''-hours with sorrow chide us not!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., ii. 6.}}
<poem>To ''after''-age thou shalt be writ the man,
That with smooth air couldst humour best our tongue.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Sonnets, viii.}}
<poem>Wheresoever I am sung or told
In ''after''-time, this also shall be known.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Morte d’Arthur.}}}}
'''2.''' ''Naut.'': (''a'') Further aft, or toward the stern of the ship: as, the ''after''-sails; the ''after''-hatchway. (''b'') Pertaining to the after-body of a ship: as, ''after''-timbers.—'''After-cabin, after-peak, after-sail, after-yard.''' {{smaller|See the respective nouns.}}

'''afterbirth''' (ȧf′tėr-bėrth), ''n.'' '''1.''' That which is expelled from the uterus after the birth of a child. It includes the placenta, part of the umbilical cord, and the membranes of the ovum. Also called ''secundines''.—'''2.''' A posthu-

